H11 run at last: reblocking removes every spill and does not move the wall clock
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), native Windows, mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2,
llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201) + XRT SDK. The A/B took a hardware context, one arm at a time.
`xrt-smi examine -r aie-partitions` reported no foreign contexts beforehand.

Commands (from the repo root, ironenv, XRT SDK on PATH and pyxrt on PYTHONPATH):
  python kernels/gemm_reblock/build_reblocked.py --cache-home <dir>            # reblocked
  python kernels/gemm_reblock/build_reblocked.py --cache-home <dir> --stock    # upstream
  python kernels/gemm_reblock/reblock_ab_test.py --repeats 4 --iters 40

WHY. H11 was proposed twice in this repo and never run. mm.cc dispatches int8->int32 to
matmul_vectorized_4x2_mmul, which holds EIGHT live 4x8x8 accumulators; five is this machine's
measured spill-free ceiling, the compiled object spills twelve vector values into a 416-byte
frame, and results/aie/accumulator_width_vs_count.log showed the bf16 path at the same total
accumulator width spilling nothing at all. mm.cc already contains a 2x2 template holding four.
The change is two lines in one wrapper.

HOW IT WAS BUILT WITHOUT TOUCHING SHARED STATE. kernels.mm() takes no source override; the
path resolves into the installed toolchain, which every session on this machine shares. So the
reblocked mm.cc lives in kernels/gemm_reblock/aie2/, and build_reblocked.py redirects the one
lookup in-process. Nothing outside this repo is modified.

A TRAP WORTH RECORDING, BECAUSE ITS ONLY SYMPTOM IS SILENCE. The first attempt appeared to
succeed -- it compiled, verified, and timed -- while actually running the STOCK kernel.
@iron.jit keys its cache on the design and its compile-time arguments, NOT on the kernel
source, so the reblocked build found a matching cache entry and reused the whole xclbin
without recompiling anything. The only evidence was that no new .o appeared on disk. Each arm
therefore gets its own NPU_CACHE_HOME, and the A/B driver requires it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT THE CHANGE DID TO THE OBJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Stock, matmul_vectorized_4x2_mmul, 8 live accumulators:

== C:\Users\<user>/.npu/cache/e9ed1c603d42d8bb153dfb76/matmul_i8_i32_86901378.o
  section .text.matmul_i8_i32: 144 bundles (2 full-width, 142 compressed), frame 416 B, stack refs 33
      acc registers (9): cm0 cm1 cm2 cm3 cm4 cm5 cm6 cm7 cm8
      vec registers (12): x0 x1 x2 x3 x4 x5 x6 x7 x8 x9 x10 x11
    loop .L_LEnd0 0x1d0-0x220: 9 bundles = 9 cycles/iteration, 25 live ops, density 2.78 of 6
  
Reblocked, matmul_vectorized_2x2_mmul, 4 live accumulators:

== C:\Users\<user>/.claude/jobs/8a20f3f5/tmp/abcache\reblock\9ea9ae1823bc2a5e0dd42395\matmul_i8_i32_4e833b59.o
  section .text.matmul_i8_i32: 88 bundles (9 full-width, 79 compressed), frame 32 B, stack refs 5
      acc registers (8): cm0 cm1 cm2 cm3 cm4 cm5 cm6 cm7
      vec registers (9): x0 x1 x2 x3 x4 x5 x6 x8 x10
    loop .L_LEnd0 0x130-0x190: 8 bundles = 8 cycles/iteration, 25 live ops, density 3.12 of 6
  
  metric                      stock 4x2        reblocked 2x2
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  bundles in the function           144                   88
  stack frame                     416 B                 32 B
  stack references                   33                    5
  hardware loop                 9 bundles            8 bundles
  vmac in that loop                   8                    8
  MAC issue rate                  0.889/cyc          1.000/cyc

Every static metric improves, and the spill is gone: twelve vector spill slots become none.
The reblocked loop issues one MAC every cycle, which is the ceiling.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE PAIRED MEASUREMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Arms alternate, and the order reverses on alternate repeats. Each arm builds and runs from
its own cache, so neither can reuse the other's compile.

shape 4096x2048x2048  tile 64/64/64  4 columns  int8->int32
4 alternating repeats per arm, 5 warmup + 40 timed iterations each
caches: C:\Users\<user>\.claude\jobs\8a20f3f5\tmp\abcache

 rep arm           avg us     min us     max us    GFLOPS  verify
------------------------------------------------------------
   1 stock         7911.6     7624.9     8354.6    4342.9  PASS
   1 reblock       7856.4     7580.7     8154.5    4373.5  PASS
   2 reblock       7758.0     7577.5     8153.0    4428.9  PASS
   2 stock         7873.8     7510.9     8455.8    4363.8  PASS
   3 stock         7907.4     7484.1     8304.1    4345.3  PASS
   3 reblock       7860.4     7714.7     8378.3    4371.2  PASS
   4 reblock       7884.8     7543.6     9249.1    4357.7  PASS
   4 stock         7866.3     7390.3     8702.8    4368.0  PASS

arm          best us   median avg  best GFLOPS  n
-------------------------------------------------
stock         7390.3       7890.6       4368.0  4
reblock       7543.6       7858.4       4428.9  4

  best-to-best   reblock is -2.1% vs stock
  median-to-med  reblock is +0.4% vs stock

  Positive means the reblocked kernel is FASTER. Its inner loop issues
  1.000 vmac/cycle against stock's 0.889 and it spills nothing, so an
  issue-bound design should show roughly +12%. The per-buffer floor
  reading predicts approximately 0%.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE KERNEL GOT STRICTLY BETTER AND THE DISPATCH BARELY MOVED. Static MAC issue rate rises
from 0.889 to 1.000 per cycle, a 12.5% improvement, and every accumulator spill disappears.
An issue-bound design should show roughly +12% on the wall clock. Two A/B series were run,
the one above and one before it:

    series          best-to-best   median-to-median
    exploratory        +0.8%            +2.0%
    captured above     -2.1%            +0.4%

Positive means the reblocked kernel is faster. The effect is inside +/-2% and its sign is not
even stable between series, against a predicted +12%.

THIS IS THE TEST H12 FAILED TO BE. results/aie/bank_ab_h12_npu.log tried to settle whether
this design is bound by its core or by a per-buffer delivery floor, using a bank-placement
change worth about 3% -- and could not resolve it, because the machine's own run-to-run drift
is about 5%. This intervention is worth four times as much, its static improvement is
unambiguous and multi-dimensional rather than a single cycle per iteration -- and the wall
clock still refuses to move. That is a stronger result than H12 reached: not "we could not
resolve a 3% effect", but "a 12.5% improvement in the kernel produced no measurable change".
The core is not the critical path, and the per-buffer floor that
results/aie/gemm_cost_model_nest.log inferred is now supported by an intervention large enough
that its absence is itself the evidence.

TAKE THE CHANGE ANYWAY, BUT STOP EXPECTING WALL CLOCK FROM KERNEL CODE HERE. The two lines
are free and strictly better: fewer bundles, no spills, a full MAC every cycle, no correctness
cost, every run verified. Keep it. But it converts a 12.5% improvement in the kernel into
nothing measurable, and THAT is the finding. On this design, at this tile, further work on the
inner loop is close to worthless -- which is what the cost model said before this kernel was
built, and this is the first time that prediction has been checked against a real kernel
change rather than against itself.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- One shape (4096x2048x2048), one tile (64/64/64), one dtype, four columns, one machine, one
  sitting. The 2x2 blocking's own trade -- half the accumulators means twice the accumulator
  groups per call -- was not swept across tiles, and a different tile could favour it more or
  less.
- +0.8% and +2.0% are within this machine's drift, which the H12 log measured at about 5% for
  repeats of an identical build. The claim made here is NOT that 2% is real; it is that the
  measured change is far below the ~12% an issue-bound design would show. That is a bound, not
  a point estimate.
- Correctness is the design's own numpy check on every run, which for integer dtypes is exact
  equality. It is not an accuracy evaluation on a model.
- The static comparison is between two objects built from the same source file differing in
  one wrapper's dispatch, but they were compiled into different cache roots by different
  processes; the compiler version and flags are identical.
- Nothing here says the 2x2 blocking is better in general. It is better on this kernel, at
  this tile, on this silicon, by the static measures listed.
